(a) Establishment of wellhead protection zone. There is hereby established a use district known as a "wellhead protection zone", identified and described as all area within a circle the center of which is the center of any county water supply wellhead and the radius of which is five hundred (500) feet.
(b) Permitted uses. The following uses shall be permitted within wellhead protection zones:
(1) Any use permitted within existing agricultural or single family districts, except that the minimum residential lot size for a lot any portion of which lies within the wellhead protection zone shall not be less than one (1) acre; and
(2) Any other open land use where any building located on the property is incidental and accessory to the primary open land use.
(c) Prohibited uses. The following uses of conditions shall be and are hereby prohibited within wellhead protection zones, whether or not such use or condition may otherwise be ordinarily included as a part of a use permitted under subsection (b) of this section:
(1) Surface use or storage of hazardous material, expressly including commercial use of agricultural pesticides;
(2) Septic tanks or drain fields appurtenant thereto;
(3) Impervious surfaces other than roofs of buildings and street, and driveways and walks serving building permitted under subsection (b) of this section;
(4) Sanitary landfills;
(5) Hazardous waste disposal sites;
(6) Storm water infiltration basins;
(7) Underground storage tanks; and
(8) Sanitary sewer lines within one hundred fifty (150) feet of a wellhead.
(d) Administration. The policies and procedures for administration of any wellhead protection zone established under this section, including without limitation those applicable to nonconforming uses, exceptions, enforcement and penalties, shall be the same as provided in the existing zoning ordinance for the county, as the same is presently enacted or may from time to time be amended.
(Ord. No. 2007-004A, § II, 8-28-07)

501:
(a) The tax commissioner [is authorized] to exclude the following categories of properties from the transfer of executions pursuant to O.C.G.A. § 48-3-19(d).
(1) Mapping problems being worked with the tax assessor;
(2) Property that has applied for exemption;
(3) Bankrupt taxpayer's property;
(4) Property involved in a year's support case;
(5) Extreme hardship cases;
(6) Property valuation under appeal;
(7) Title search and/or levy action in progress prior to tax sale;
(8) Property that has been advertised for public auction;
(9) Property that involves legal problems and/or questions as determined by the county attorney;
(10) Property that has received homestead exemptions; and
(11) Property that, in the opinion of the tax commissioner and as approved by a majority vote of the county commission, would not be in the best interest of the county to sell for tax execution collection.
(b) If, in the opinion of the tax commissioner, as approved by a majority of the county commission, certain of the above exempted property is determined in the best interest of the county to be allowed for sale as provided by O.C.G.A. § 48-3-19(c), then said tax executions may be transferred for sale pursuant to said Code section.
(Mo. of 8-27-96)
